WEBVTT


00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.360
With just a few steps, you can add
beautiful buttonholes to your doubled

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.400
button bands.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.055
Be sure to watch this entire video.

00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:21.455
We'll go through the steps and then go
back to see how we can improve on our

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:22.175
button holes.

00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:28.805
Now this takes practice, so please
practice before starting on your sweater.

00:00:29.555 --> 00:00:34.565
In addition, we're assuming that you're
familiar with picking up and knitting a

00:00:34.565 --> 00:00:41.975
doubled band . We'll be knitting an 8 row
band with a turning row and three stitch

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:42.635
buttonholes.

00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:51.395
Start by pulling out the needles that you
need for your band and open the latches to

00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:52.685
make things easier.

00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:59.505
With the private or the wrong side facing
rehang your garment edge on the machine.

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:01.145
At

00:01:10.764 --> 00:01:13.724
garment tension, knit 4 rows.

00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:21.115
These are the sets of buttonhole
needles that I needed.

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:29.679
Mark the needle bed, so it will be easier
to identify the correct needles as you

00:01:29.679 --> 00:01:31.059
form the buttonholes.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.130
Next, put in some provisional stitches.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:38.649
That will be the start
of the button holes.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:47.285
Using Ravel cord, use a contrasting color,
knit by hand, each set of three stitches.

00:01:48.485 --> 00:01:50.675
You can control those ends with clips.

00:01:53.765 --> 00:01:59.134
Still knitting at garment tension, knit
another four rows to finish the first half

00:01:59.134 --> 00:01:59.825
of the band.

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:01.985
Knit a turning row.

00:02:02.975 --> 00:02:06.725
I just chose to loosen my carriage
tension to 10 for this row.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.270
Starting knitting the
second half of the band.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.400
Don't forget to adjust your tension.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:19.589
You'll want to knit the second half of the
band at garment tension minus one and knit

00:02:19.619 --> 00:02:20.760
4 rows.

00:02:21.769 --> 00:02:24.560
This is the first half
of this side of the band.

00:02:25.549 --> 00:02:27.049
Now the fun starts.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.200
We're going to pick up the bars at
the top of our provisional stitches.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.970
Pull out the first set
of three marked needles.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.500
To correctly form the buttonhole.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:48.250
You'll be picking up four bars, doubling
up on the three buttonhole needles, plus

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.060
one more.

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:52.590
Do this for all the buttonholes.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.990
Now look closely.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.750
Those provisional stitches are still in
place and they're still holding 3 live

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.960
stitches that will form the
other half of the buttonhole.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.450
Pick up the top bars for
each of the buttonholes,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:19.250
placing the strands on the
marked needles plus one more.

00:03:27.424 --> 00:03:30.905
Next we'll bind off to
secure those stitches.

00:03:36.424 --> 00:03:41.434
Using a separate strand of yarn and the
method of your choice, bind off these

00:03:41.434 --> 00:03:42.095
stitches.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.790
Secure the last bind off
stitch on the adjacent needle.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.750
Your buttonhole needles are now empty.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.299
Don't panic.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.750
The holes look really big and
sloppy, but we're not finished yet.

00:04:13.714 --> 00:04:17.135
Continue binding off for
each one of the buttonholes.

00:04:34.825 --> 00:04:39.235
Secure those bind off strands by
wrapping them around the edge needles.

00:04:40.315 --> 00:04:45.775
I like to hang clips to tighten
up slightly and secure those ends.

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:13.315
Remember those three live stitches
that are being held below.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.840
Now's the time to pick them
up and finish the buttonhole.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.440
Identify each stitch and hang it
on the corresponding empty needle.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:30.380
Do this for each one of the
sets of buttonholes needles.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:55.510
Still knitting at your garment
tension minus one knit 4 rows.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.840
The knitting of your band is complete.

00:06:03.945 --> 00:06:08.775
Before we close up the band, you can
remove the ravel cord provisional stitches

00:06:09.375 --> 00:06:12.675
and finish off and work
in those bind off strands.

00:06:13.935 --> 00:06:15.735
Now please keep watching this video.

00:06:15.735 --> 00:06:20.835
I'll talk about my boo boo that I made
here and some other options for this

00:06:20.985 --> 00:06:22.605
entire technique in a minute.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:39.350
Buttonholes are finished.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:41.060
The band knitting is finished.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.500
Just need to secure the
band to the garment edge.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.480
Now there are a lot of ways to do this.

00:06:54.735 --> 00:06:59.145
I chose to pick up the garment
edge and then just simply bind off.

00:07:08.125 --> 00:07:13.255
Each side of the band is secured at the
buttonhole edges and the finish is nice

00:07:13.255 --> 00:07:13.675
and neat.

00:07:15.065 --> 00:07:18.605
Now right off the machine,
the band seems distorted.

00:07:19.135 --> 00:07:22.865
These are pretty big button holes
and they're fairly close together.

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.745
Once blocked, this band will be beautiful.

00:07:26.915 --> 00:07:29.225
Well, let's go back and
look at some options.

00:07:30.005 --> 00:07:34.205
I'm going to refer to some companion
videos that will help with this technique.

00:07:34.625 --> 00:07:38.645
The videos can be found in the
Knit it Now Buttonhole Classroom.

00:07:39.875 --> 00:07:44.915
When you pick up the garment edge,
consider picking up a single loop for less

00:07:44.915 --> 00:07:45.545
bulk.

00:07:46.175 --> 00:07:49.025
Watch the video Seaming Stitches to Rows.

00:08:01.305 --> 00:08:06.345
Be sure to practice picking up and
knitting plain bands before adding

00:08:06.345 --> 00:08:10.185
buttonholes .Check the
videos in the classroom.

00:08:12.065 --> 00:08:17.645
If you're having trouble picturing which
stitches to pick up, or if you're asking:

00:08:17.645 --> 00:08:21.845
" why do you pick up four stitches in
the first part of this technique", please

00:08:21.845 --> 00:08:23.855
watch the video "Upside Down

00:08:23.855 --> 00:08:24.695
Stitches."

00:08:27.245 --> 00:08:32.284
I used a stitch-through-stitch bind off
to close one side of the button hole.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:55.330
And you can see, the chains that
are formed with that bind off.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:09:00.620
If you're a perfectionist, consider
joining the stitches with Kitchener stitch

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:04.460
or a backstitch bind off that
might not be as noticeable.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.210
Check the bind off
videos in the classroom.

00:09:09.865 --> 00:09:14.255
Using a separate strand of yarn to bind
off the buttonholes proved to be a little

00:09:14.255 --> 00:09:15.245
problematic.

00:09:16.025 --> 00:09:21.515
I was kind of lazy and I just wrapped the
loose strands around the end needles, but

00:09:21.515 --> 00:09:25.055
I really didn't take any time
to secure those loose ends.

00:09:25.595 --> 00:09:27.545
I found that the strands popped out.

00:09:28.565 --> 00:09:30.845
Maybe I should have left a longer tail.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:38.200
But a better solution would be to use a
bridging technique and use the main yarn.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:45.310
In other words, when it came to bind off
the stitches, put all the needles to hold.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:48.790
Knit the first set of needles.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.700
And then free pass back.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:56.530
Use the main yarn to bind off
those buttonhole stitches.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:59.650
Knit the second set of needles.

00:10:00.185 --> 00:10:06.725
Free pass back, use the main yarn to bind
off the second button hole and continue

00:10:06.725 --> 00:10:08.105
this across the row.

00:10:08.825 --> 00:10:14.645
So instead of using loose strands, you
can use the actual yarn of the garment.

00:10:16.175 --> 00:10:19.895
Although this is tedious, it would
really eliminate those loose ends.

00:10:21.725 --> 00:10:25.265
The last thoughts on this
technique is more about the bands.

00:10:26.165 --> 00:10:30.305
As I mentioned before, you need to be
familiar with picking up and knitting

00:10:30.305 --> 00:10:30.875
bands.

00:10:31.445 --> 00:10:33.485
There are many techniques for this.

00:10:34.205 --> 00:10:39.365
Please watch the video band perfection for
some different methods that can be used

00:10:39.365 --> 00:10:41.015
with this buttonhole technique.

00:10:46.655 --> 00:10:50.105
A reversible band could also
use this button hole method.

00:10:51.945 --> 00:10:56.295
Add your provisional Ravel cord to
one side and work the button hole.

00:10:56.295 --> 00:10:58.485
As you knit the second side of the band.

00:11:00.635 --> 00:11:05.225
Please take time to master this technique
and try some of these suggestions

00:11:05.225 --> 00:11:05.825
yourself.

00:11:06.665 --> 00:11:11.735
By perfecting your button holes, you will
take your knitting from looking homemade

00:11:12.435 --> 00:11:14.015
looking handcrafted.

